    pv_sections: [{"h": "API keys", "p": ["Your API keys are stored in the Windows Credential Manager (one entry per brain: gemini-api-key, groq-api-key, claude-api-key and openai-api-key, service streakboom) and are sent only to the provider you chose, in a request header. They are never written to a file or sent to any other window of the app."]}, {"h": "What the AI provider receives", "p": ["With Google's free tier, what you send may be used and read by Google, and API users must be 18 or older (clients in the EEA, Switzerland and the UK must use paid services). For Groq, Claude and OpenAI, what you send goes to Groq (which runs the model), Anthropic or OpenAI to be answered; see their terms. The app says so before you connect.", "Without a key the pet does not call any AI provider.", "After Peak falls, a connected brain may add one short remark (Settings, Brain, on by default). What is sent is only the name of the event and five numbers about how it feels, never anything about your windows or screen, and it counts toward the daily limit of unprompted lines."]}, {"h": "What Peak remembers", "p": ["Chats are kept in memory only. What the pet remembers is stored on your computer in memory.json: the names you gave, coarse habit counts (for example how many sessions began in each hour of the day), touch counts, milestones and the short facts it noted from your chats. The facts are sent to the brain you use, together with later chats you start.", "You can see, edit, delete or switch off all of it in Settings, Memory."]}, {"h": "Usage stats (off by default)", "p": ["If you opt in, the app records funnel event names (for example “opened the chat”) and timestamps with a random id, in a local queue. Nothing is sent automatically: Settings, Privacy, Copy stats puts it on your clipboard so you can share it by hand. Opting out deletes the id and the queue.", "A second switch, Daily activity counts, adds a few numbers per day (scratches, rubs, pokes, messages sent, lines the pet said and you reacted to, minutes the app ran, times Quiet mode was switched on): only counts, never text. Turning the switch off deletes the numbers."]}, {"h": "What Peak notices around it", "p": ["To keep out of your way, Peak reads only when you last used the keyboard or the mouse (one number, never which keys), where the pointer is, and whether the primary mouse button (the one you click with) is pressed (so it remembers where you last clicked and can guess which row you are typing on). Since 0.3.1 it also asks the system what kind of thing is just under its feet (the bare desktop, the taskbar, or some window) and, only while it stands there, keeps an opaque number that means the same window as a moment ago, so it can notice that the window it stands on was closed, minimized or dragged away. A window's class name is looked at only to tell the desktop and the taskbar from other windows and is dropped at once.", "Since 0.3.2 it also keeps a coarse picture of where windows are: a grid of squares, each the bare desktop, the taskbar or some window, with an anonymous number that says which squares are the same window. It is on by default, kept in memory only, never saved or sent, and has no titles, names or contents; you switch it off in Settings, Privacy. Peak uses it to find the top edge of a window to sit on and the side of a window to climb. When the Start menu or Search opens over Peak, it asks the system whether something that it cannot be raised over is covering a few points of its body (only \"covered or not\") and, if so, hops out.", "It never reads what you type, which keys you press, what is on your screen, window titles or app names. These readings are kept only in memory, are never written to a file or sent anywhere, and are gone when Peak closes."]}, {"h": "Files on your computer", "p": ["Files live in %APPDATA%\\com.streakboom.app\\: state.json (window position, Quiet mode, the brain in use and the chosen models), pet.json (everything else about the pet) and memory.json (what it remembers)."]}, {"h": "This website", "p": ["This website sets no cookies and has no analytics. If your browser's language is Vietnamese, Japanese or English and the page is in another of them, it may show a thin line that offers the same page in your language. It never moves you by itself. If you close that line or follow it, the language is saved in this browser only, so the line does not come back; nothing is sent anywhere. The sticker page keeps the stickers you used last in the same way, and Clear removes them."]}, {"h": "Updates", "p": ["Unless you choose “never check”, the app reads a small update manifest from the project's GitHub repository (and the installer from GitHub). An update is only installed if its signature verifies."]}],
